  ],
  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This is a flat scarf worked from one short end to the other in "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "basketweave-stitch"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "basketweave stitch"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " on 5 mm needles with aran-weight yarn, finishing at 22 cm wide and 175 cm long. The six-stitch blocks of knit and purl squares shift position every six rows, creating an interlocked grid that reads as woven fabric."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Flat construction was chosen because basketweave's block pattern is worked in rows, shifting the knit and purl blocks by half a repeat after every six rows. A row counter is strongly recommended to track position in the repeat. The scarf uses about 210 g (378 m / 413 yards) of aran-weight yarn. The chart above shows the 12-stitch, 12-row repeat."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "suppliesCard",
        "attrs": {
          "heading": "Materials",
          "items": [
            {
              "name": "Aran-weight wool or wool-blend yarn",
              "qty": "210 g (approx. 378 m / 413 yards)",
              "substitutions": "Any aran yarn. Solid or semi-solid colours show the block texture clearly."
            },
            {
              "name": "5 mm needles (UK 6 / US 8)",
              "qty": "1 pair or 1 circular",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Row counter",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": "A pencil and paper work equally well."
            },
            {
              "name": "Tapestry or darning needle",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Scissors",
              "qty": "1 pair",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Measuring tape",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gauge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "18 sts x 24 rows = 10 x 10 cm in basketweave stitch on 5 mm needles, blocked. Each stitch off gauge shifts the finished width by about 1.2 cm; each two rows off gauge across the full 420-row scarf adds or removes about 7 cm of finished length."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitches used"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "K (knit), P (purl). The pattern places blocks of six knit stitches alongside blocks of six purl stitches, and shifts their positions every six rows."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pattern"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast on"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Use the "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "long-tail-cast-on"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "long-tail cast-on"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " to place 40 stitches. (38 pattern stitches in a multiple of 6, plus 1 selvedge stitch each side.) Tail length for 5 mm needles and 40 stitches: (15.7 x 40) / 10 + 15 = about 78 cm."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitch count check-in: 40 stitches after the cast-on."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Body"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Rows 1-6 (RS, then alternate): K1 (selvedge), (K6, P6) 3 times, K7. (40 sts)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Rows 7-12: K1 (selvedge), (P6, K6) 3 times, P6, K1. (40 sts)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Note: on WS rows, knit what appears as a purl stitch from the RS and purl what appears as a knit stitch. The chart shows all stitches as they appear from the right side. Repeat the 12-row pattern until the scarf measures 175 cm from the cast-on edge. At the halfway mark (about 87 cm), count: still 40 stitches."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast off"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast off on a Row 1 (RS) of the pattern using the standard method. Work the cast-off in pattern (knit the knit stitches as they appear). Work at normal tension."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Common faults"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Blocks shifting at the wrong row. The most common error is starting the second half of the repeat too early or too late. Use a row counter and mark Row 7 explicitly when you shift from (K6, P6) to (P6, K6)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Knit columns appearing in the purl blocks. On WS rows the instruction is to work the stitches as they appear from the right side, which means you knit what looks like a purl and purl what looks like a knit when the work is facing you. This is the one step where reading the stitches on the needle, rather than the written instruction, saves errors."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitch count changing. Basketweave does not generate unintentional yarn-overs as readily as rib, but dropped stitches at block boundaries are common. Count after every 12-row repeat until the pattern is familiar."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finishing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weave in ends. Wet blocking evens out the blocks: soak in cool water, press out moisture, and pin flat. The basketweave pattern benefits from a gentle block."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Care"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Handwash wool and lay flat to dry. Cotton: gentle machine wash, lay flat. Acrylic: cool gentle machine wash, no tumble dry."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What to try next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The simple cable scarf uses the same aran weight but adds the extra step of crossing stitches with a cable needle to produce twisted columns. Or try the colour-block scarf for a beginner stripes project."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}